The End of Programmed Robotics

From Code to Cognition. From Algorithms to Doctrine.

⸻

The Vision

Robotic autonomy should no longer be built primarily through procedural programming, but instead through doctrine, knowledge, and reasoning.

Our objective is to progressively replace traditional procedural programming with a cognitive architecture governed by high-level operational manuals, dynamically interpreted and executed through Large Language Models (LLMs) and Vision-Language Models (VLMs).

In this paradigm, perception interprets the environment, cognitive layers reason about the state of the world and mission objectives, and physical control translates decisions into action under deterministic safety constraints.

Robotic behavior is no longer exhaustively prescribed by code; instead, it emerges from the contextual application of principles, objectives, procedures, and decision-making criteria expressed in natural language.

Our ambition is not merely to integrate artificial intelligence into conventional robotics, but to fundamentally invert its foundations: replacing behavioral programming with doctrinal specification, and the execution of predefined algorithms with situated cognitive reasoning.

The Fundamental Principle

Code implements physical capabilities and safety guarantees. Doctrine defines how intelligence should be applied to make decisions.

This distinction preserves the rigor of robotic control while freeing robotic intelligence from the need to encode every new behavior as a procedural software development effort.

The goal is not to eliminate software from robotics, but to end the assumption that intelligent robotic behavior must be explicitly programmed.

